@page "/editor"

<CatHead Title="Rich-text editor" Tier="@CatHead.Tier3">
    A document a reader writes with formatting &mdash; a note, a description, a reply &mdash; posted as
    HTML. Your markup is the frame, the toolbar, the link and media forms and a <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> that
    holds the value; the library ships the engine and loads it the first time a page shows an editor.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Editors">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.Note" Title="Note editor" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; type, format, add a link with <kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">K</kbd>.</strong>
            <code>[data-editor]</code> on the <code>.editor</code> frame makes it one. The document is edited in
            <code>[data-editor-body]</code>; put <code>.prose</code> on it so what is typed looks like what is later
            shown. <code>textarea[data-editor-value]</code> is the field: the editor loads from it and writes every
            change back to it, as HTML, so a form posts it and a Blazor binding reads it. Keep it
            <code>hidden</code>. The editable element takes the textarea's label, its placeholder and its state.
        </p>
        <p>
            A button with <code>data-editor-format</code> toggles its format on the selection and shows, with
            <code>aria-pressed</code>, what the text under the caret has. A button pressed with the pointer leaves
            the focus and the selection in the document.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.Everything" Title="Every control" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>The formats an editor accepts are the ones its toolbar offers.</strong> Paste a page in here:
            what it had beyond these is cut away, so the document never holds what the reader could not have
            written. A format with a <code>value</code> sets that value &mdash; a heading level, a kind of list,
            an alignment &mdash; and a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> sets the option chosen, with
            <code>""</code> for a paragraph. <code>data-editor-action</code> buttons undo, redo, indent,
            outdent and clear the formatting; undo and redo are <code>disabled</code> while there is nothing to do.
        </p>
        <p>
            The toolbar is one tab stop: <kbd class="kbd">←</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">→</kbd>, <kbd class="kbd">Home</kbd>
            and <kbd class="kbd">End</kbd> move between its buttons, <kbd class="kbd">Alt</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">F10</kbd>
            in the document goes to it and <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> comes back. <kbd class="kbd">Tab</kbd> in the
            document leaves it, as from a textarea &mdash; except in a list or a code block, where it indents.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.Comment" Title="Comment field" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            Four buttons, four formats: a comment cannot be given a heading or a list, however it is pasted in.
            <code>--editor-max-height</code> on the frame caps how tall the document grows before it scrolls, and
            the textarea's <code>placeholder</code> is what an empty one says.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.ReadOnly" Title="Read-only" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <code>readonly</code> or <code>disabled</code> on the textarea stops the editing, dims the toolbar and
            marks the document <code>aria-readonly</code>. The editor follows the attribute when it changes, so a
            Blazor binding to <code>disabled</code> needs no call.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Menus and links">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.Slash" Title="Slash menu" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; type <kbd class="kbd">/</kbd> at the start of a line.</strong> Write a
            <code>.menu.editor-slash</code> with <code>data-editor-slash</code> inside the editor, one
            <code>.menu-item</code> per kind of block with <code>data-editor-block</code> and a <code>value</code>, as
            a toolbar button names its format. What follows the <kbd class="kbd">/</kbd> filters it; the focus stays in
            the document, which points at the lit item. <kbd class="kbd">&uarr;</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">&darr;</kbd>
            choose, <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> turns the line into it and removes what was typed,
            <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> closes it.
        </p>
        <p>
            The menu offers only what the toolbar does: an item whose format the editor does not accept is never
            shown. <code>paragraph</code> takes every block format off the line. Give the items
            <code>tabindex="-1"</code>, so the menu is never a Tab stop.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <h3 id="link-form">Link form <CatAnchor Id="link-form" /></h3>
    <p class="cat-lede">
        Your own form, written once inside the editor as <code>.editor-link[data-editor-link]</code>, which the
        script opens beneath the text it is for &mdash; from a <code>data-editor-format="link"</code> button or
        <kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">K</kbd>. It is filled with the address of the link under
        the caret, and <code>[data-editor-link-remove]</code> shows only then. <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> saves,
        <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> closes, and <code>[data-editor-link-cancel]</code> closes too. With no text
        selected, the address is written as the link's text. Only <code>http</code>, <code>https</code>,
        <code>mailto</code>, <code>tel</code> and <code>sms</code> addresses are kept.
    </p>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Media">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.Media" Title="Images and videos" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; put the caret on a line and use the image or video button.</strong>
            <code>data-editor-format="image"</code> and <code>"video"</code> open your own form,
            <code>.editor-media[data-editor-media]</code>, written once inside the editor and placed at the caret
            like the link form: <code>[data-editor-media-url]</code> takes the address,
            <code>[data-editor-media-alt]</code> the description &mdash; an image's <code>alt</code>, a video's
            <code>aria-label</code> &mdash; and <code>[data-editor-media-save]</code> inserts. The form says which it
            is for in <code>data-editor-media-kind</code>, and shows an element marked
            <code>data-editor-media-for="image"</code> or <code>"video"</code> only for that kind. Opened on an image
            or a video, it is filled with it, and Insert replaces it. <strong>A click on an image or a video in the
            document chooses it</strong> &mdash; an outline in the focus ring marks it, set as
            <code>data-editor-selected</code> &mdash; so the control then changes that media instead of adding a
            second. A video's own controls keep their clicks: click its frame, or press Shift+Arrow from the line
            before it or after it. Typing or a click elsewhere lets it go.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>The bar.</strong> Write <code>.editor-media-bar[data-editor-media-bar]</code> once inside the
            editor, with <code>hidden</code>, and the editor shows it above the chosen media and hides it when it is let
            go. Its controls are yours: <code>data-editor-media-size="240"</code> sets the width in pixels and clears the
            height, <code>""</code> is the natural size; <code>data-editor-media-align="start"</code>, <code>"center"</code>,
            <code>"end"</code> or <code>""</code> sets <code>data-align</code>; <code>data-editor-media-edit</code> opens the
            media form on it and <code>data-editor-media-remove</code> deletes it. Size and align controls carry
            <code>aria-pressed</code>; an element marked <code>data-editor-media-for="image"</code> shows for that kind
            only. <kbd class="kbd">Alt</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">F10</kbd> goes to the bar while a media is chosen, the
            arrows move between its controls, and <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> comes back to the document.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>How a picture sits.</strong> <code>data-align</code> is written on the <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> or the
            <code>&lt;video&gt;</code> and kept through paste and load; any other value is dropped. <code>start</code> and
            <code>end</code> float a picture to that edge with the text beside it, <code>center</code> makes it a centred
            block, and <code>.prose</code> styles all three wherever the HTML is shown &mdash; mirrored in a right-to-left
            block, and stacked below a phone's width. It is an attribute rather than a class so the HTML stays plain.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>[data-editor-media-width]</code> and <code>[data-editor-media-height]</code> are the size, in
            pixels, as the <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> attributes &mdash; for an image and a video
            alike. Both are optional, and <strong>empty removes the attribute</strong>, so the browser keeps the
            aspect ratio from the one that is left. A value that is not a whole number above zero marks its field
            <code>aria-invalid</code> and leaves the form open. A form without the two fields leaves the size of the
            media it replaces as it was.
        </p>
        <p>
            An image sits in its line as <code>&lt;img src alt&gt;</code>; a video is a block of its own,
            <code>&lt;video controls src&gt;</code>, which never plays by itself. Both are formats like any other: an
            editor whose toolbar offers neither cuts them out of what is pasted. Only <code>http</code>,
            <code>https</code> and relative addresses are kept &mdash; a <code>data:</code> image is dropped, and a
            file pasted or dropped into the document is not inlined. <strong>Uploading is the app's</strong>: put the
            file where it can be fetched, and hand the editor its address.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.FileNotice" Title="Refused pictures" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; paste or drop a screenshot into the note.</strong> The editor never inlines a file,
            so in a field that takes no pictures one would vanish without a word. Write the words once, inside the
            editor, as <code>.editor-notice[data-editor-file-notice]</code> with <code>hidden</code>: a strip at the
            foot of the frame, coloured by <code>.form-warning</code> and announced by <code>role="alert"</code>.
            The editor shows it when a pasted or dropped file is not taken, and hides it at the next change to the
            document.
        </p>
        <p>
            Taken means the app cancelled <code>sedna-editor-file</code> &mdash;
            <code>@@onsedna-editor-file:preventDefault</code> beside the handler that uploads it, or
            <code>preventDefault()</code> in a listener. A file that is not an image or a video the editor knows
            sends no event and always shows the notice. An editor without the notice is unchanged.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.EditorImagePicker" Title="Your own image picker" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; put the caret in the report, then add a photo.</strong> Before the media form opens,
            the editor sends <code>sedna-editor-media</code> with <code>{ kind, src, alt, width, height }</code>. Cancel it with
            <code>@@onsedna-editor-media:preventDefault</code> and the form stays shut: open your own dialog &mdash;
            an upload, a media library &mdash; and answer with
            <code>ISednaEditors.InsertImageAsync(id, url, alt)</code> or <code>InsertVideoAsync(id, url, label)</code>,
            which put the media where the caret was and answer null when the address is not one the editor keeps.
            From script, it is <code>insertImage(url, alt)</code> and <code>insertVideo(url, label)</code> on the
            editor's handle. Each takes a width and a height in pixels after the description &mdash; in C#, an
            overload with an <code>int?</code> each &mdash; and without them keeps the size of the media it
            replaces; <code>null</code> removes it.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Writing">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.Shortcuts" Title="Markdown shortcuts and rules" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; type <kbd class="kbd">#</kbd> and a space, <kbd class="kbd">&gt;</kbd> and a space,
            <kbd class="kbd">---</kbd> and <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd>, or <code>**bold**</code>.</strong> A reader who
            knows Markdown gets the format it names, for the formats the editor accepts and no others:
            <code>#</code> to <code>######</code> a heading, <code>&gt;</code> a quote, <code>---</code> a horizontal
            rule, three backticks a code block, and <code>**x**</code>, <code>*x*</code>, <code>`x`</code>,
            <code>~~x~~</code> bold, italic, code and strike when the closing mark is typed. A list starts with
            <code>- </code>, <code>1. </code> or <code>[ ] </code>. Each is its own step in the history:
            <kbd class="kbd">Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd class="kbd">Z</kbd> puts back what was typed. Nothing happens inside
            code.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>data-editor-action="rule"</code> puts an <code>&lt;hr&gt;</code> at the caret, in a line of its own;
            <code>.prose hr</code> draws it. <code>data-editor-format="direction"</code> with <code>value="rtl"</code>
            sets a block right to left. A link is written with <code>target="_blank"</code> and
            <code>rel="noopener noreferrer"</code>.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.Mentions" Title="Naming someone" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; type <kbd class="kbd">&#64;</kbd> after a space, then a name.</strong> The list is a
            <code>.menu.editor-mention[data-editor-mention]</code> you write once inside the editor, one
            <code>.menu-item</code> per person with <code>data-mention-value</code> and <code>data-label</code>, as the
            textarea mention list does. What follows the <kbd class="kbd">&#64;</kbd> filters it with the palette's
            matcher; <kbd class="kbd">&uarr;</kbd> <kbd class="kbd">&darr;</kbd> choose and
            <kbd class="kbd">Enter</kbd> or <kbd class="kbd">Tab</kbd> writes it, <kbd class="kbd">Esc</kbd> closes it.
        </p>
        <p>
            A mention is written as <code>&lt;data value="alex.fischer"&gt;&#64;Alex Fischer&lt;/data&gt;</code>: the value is
            the id your server resolves, the text is what the reader sees, and <code>.prose data[value]</code> colours
            it. <code>&lt;data&gt;</code> is the element HTML has for a machine-readable value, and nothing else in a
            document uses it, so it cannot be taken for another format.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.EditorMentionSearch" Title="Mentions from a search" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; type <kbd class="kbd">&#64;</kbd> and a letter.</strong> Each time the word changes the
            editor sends <code>sedna-editor-mention</code> with <code>{ query }</code>. Cancel it with
            <code>@@onsedna-editor-mention:preventDefault</code> and answer with
            <code>ISednaEditors.SetMentionsAsync(id, mentions)</code> &mdash; or <code>mentions([...])</code> from
            script &mdash; which clones one row per person from the
            <code>&lt;template data-editor-mention-item&gt;</code> in the menu, filling its <code>data-label</code>,
            <code>data-note</code> and <code>data-icon</code> slots with text. The editor then shows every row it was
            given, in the order it was given.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Limits and layout">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.EditorLimit" Title="Length limit and counter" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; type past 120 characters.</strong> <code>maxlength</code> on the textarea is the limit. The
            editor counts the document's text &mdash; what <code>state().length</code> says, not the HTML &mdash; and cuts
            what is typed or pasted beyond it, marking the frame <code>aria-invalid</code> until the next change. A
            document the app sets is its own. The counter is yours: <code>@@onsedna-editor-change</code> gives the text and
            its length, and the words are counted from the text.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.FullScreen" Title="Full screen" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; press the full-screen button.</strong> A <code>data-fullscreen</code> button with
            <code>aria-controls</code> naming the editor does it, as it does for any element; inside the toolbar it is one
            of the toolbar's stops. In full screen the document takes the height the toolbar leaves
            (<code>.editor:fullscreen</code>), and the forms and menus, which sit inside the frame, go with it.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Editor.Tables" Title="Tables" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; insert a table, then add rows and columns.</strong> Any
            <code>data-editor-action="table-insert"</code> or row and column action makes the editor take tables, and a
            stored table is kept. The insert button is <code>disabled</code> inside a table, and every other table action
            outside one. A table is drawn as a <code>.prose</code> table is &mdash; the same grid, padding and header colour &mdash; so it reads as the one the page later shows, and it fills the column so every cell is a target.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Forms and Blazor">
    <CatSnippet Name="Editor/InAForm.html" Title="In a form">
        <p>
            The textarea is the field, so a form works as it would with a textarea: it posts the document under the
            textarea's <code>name</code>, a reset puts it back, and without the script the textarea shows in the frame
            instead, still posting. <code>required</code> is held by the editor, because a browser refuses to submit
            over a hidden field it cannot focus: an empty one stops the submit, is marked <code>aria-invalid</code>
            and takes the focus. The HTML is the editor's own, cut down to the formats it accepts &mdash; a
            convenience, not a sanitiser: what arrives at the server is still the server's to check.
        </p>
    </CatSnippet>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.EditorBinding" Title="In Blazor" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; type, then use the template.</strong> <code>@@bind</code> on the textarea is the whole
            binding: the editor sends the textarea's <code>input</code> at each change and <code>change</code> when
            it loses the focus after one, so <code>@@bind</code> and <code>@@bind:event="oninput"</code> both work,
            and a value set in C# is loaded into the document. <code>@@onsedna-editor-change</code> adds the text,
            its length and whether it is empty, for a counter or a send button that waits for something to send.
        </p>
        <p>
            <code>ISednaEditors</code>, registered by <code>AddSednaUi()</code>, is for what a binding cannot do &mdash;
            type at the reader's caret, focus the document, switch it off &mdash; by the editor element's id. None of
            it runs while the page prerenders: call it from a handler, or after the first render.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Interop.EditorStateFromCsharp" Title="State from C#" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; send the step for review, then paste a screenshot into it.</strong>
            <code>GetStateAsync</code> reads what the document holds when the app needs to know &mdash; its HTML, its
            text, its length and whether it is empty &mdash; and <code>SetEnabledAsync</code> stops the reader editing,
            or lets them again; a page that renders the textarea's <code>disabled</code> needs neither call.
            <code>InsertVideoAsync(id, url, label)</code> puts a video where the caret was, here from a button of the
            app's own: <code>data-editor-formats</code> names <code>video</code>, which the toolbar does not offer.
        </p>
        <p>
            A file pasted or dropped into the document is never inlined. The editor sends
            <code>@@onsedna-editor-file</code> with an id, a name, a type and a size for each, and keeps the last few;
            <code>@@onsedna-editor-file:preventDefault</code> says the app took them, so a
            <code>[data-editor-file-notice]</code> stays hidden;
            <code>OpenFileAsync(id, fileId)</code> opens one as a stream reference, which
            <code>OpenReadStreamAsync(maxAllowedSize)</code> reads. This one counts the bytes and lists the file as an
            attachment. An app uploads them there, and answers an image meant for the text with
            <code>InsertImageAsync</code> once it has its address.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Reference">
    <h3 id="controls">Controls <CatAnchor Id="controls" /></h3>
    <div class="sedna-scroll-x">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="bold"</code>, <code>italic</code>, <code>underline</code>, <code>strike</code>, <code>code</code></td><td>Toggles the format on the selection.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="script"</code> with <code>value="sub"</code> or <code>"super"</code></td><td>Subscript, superscript.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="header"</code> with <code>value="1"</code>&ndash;<code>"6"</code>, or on a <code>&lt;select&gt;</code></td><td>A heading level; <code>""</code> is a paragraph.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="list"</code> with <code>value="bullet"</code>, <code>"ordered"</code> or <code>"check"</code></td><td>A list.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="blockquote"</code>, <code>"code-block"</code></td><td>A quote, a code block.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="align"</code> with <code>value="center"</code>, <code>"right"</code> or <code>"justify"</code></td><td>Alignment; no value is the start edge.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="link"</code></td><td>Opens the link form.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="image"</code>, <code>"video"</code></td><td>Sends <code>sedna-editor-media</code>, then opens the media form.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-action="undo"</code>, <code>"redo"</code>, <code>"indent"</code>, <code>"outdent"</code>, <code>"clean"</code></td><td>Undo, redo, one level in or out, and removing the formatting from the selection.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-format="direction"</code> with <code>value="rtl"</code></td><td>Sets the block right to left.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-action="rule"</code></td><td>A horizontal rule at the caret.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-action="table-insert"</code> and the row and column actions</td><td>Tables; see Tables.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-fullscreen</code> with <code>aria-controls</code> naming the editor</td><td>Full screen, as a toolbar stop.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-formats="bold italic link"</code> on the editor</td><td>The formats it accepts, instead of the ones its toolbar offers.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>data-editor-eager</code> on the editor</td><td>Starts it at once, rather than when it scrolls near.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3 id="events">Events <CatAnchor Id="events" /></h3>
    <div class="sedna-scroll-x">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>sedna-editor-ready</code></td><td><code>{ html, text, length, isEmpty }</code>, once the document has loaded.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sedna-editor-change</code></td><td>The same, after each change.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sedna-editor-media</code></td><td><code>{ kind, src, alt, width, height }</code>, cancelable, before the media form opens: the image or video under the caret, with its size in pixels or <code>null</code>, or empty strings and nulls.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sedna-editor-file</code></td><td><code>{ files: [{ id, name, type, size }], index }</code>, cancelable, when an image or a video file is pasted or dropped. Nothing is inserted: read each by its <code>id</code>, and cancel the event to say the app took them &mdash; otherwise the editor's <code>[data-editor-file-notice]</code> shows.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>sedna-editor-mention</code></td><td><code>{ query }</code>, cancelable, while the mention list is open: the word after the &#64;. Cancel it and answer with <code>mentions([...])</code>.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>the textarea's <code>input</code>, <code>change</code></td><td>As a textarea sends them: at each change, and when the focus leaves after one.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p class="cat-lede">
        <code>data-editor-state</code> on the editor is <code>loading</code>, then <code>ready</code>, or
        <code>error</code> when the markup has no textarea or no body. The reference is <code>docs/editor.md</code>.
    </p>
</CatGroup>
